{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789373157,"nonce":"cxCe6oCMFCRa+8ESTDDk68Q6/Ps=","title":"Python: a field or variable that may be None is a box, not a nullable pointer","message":"`bench_binary_trees.py`, depth 16, runs=3:\n\n    zynml 243 ms   zypy 4619 ms   pypy 1451 ms   cpython 14473 ms\n\nNineteen times ZynML and three times PyPy, on the one kernel that is\nabout allocation and pointer chasing. `Node(left, right, item)` stores\n`left` and `right`, and `build` passes `None` for a leaf, so inference\njoins `None` with `Class(Node)` and gets Object: the two fields are\nboxes (`*opaque(DynamicBox)` in the struct), every child is boxed on\nconstruction, `self.left is None` is a `zb_any_is` call, and\n`self.left.check()` goes through the `py$call$check$0` dispatcher,\nreturning an Object that `self.item + ...` adds with `zb_any_arith`.\nCompile is 119 ms for the same reason (the dispatchers).\n\n`Ty::Class(k)` should admit None: a class-typed value is a pointer that\nmay be null, which is what ZynML's `@reference` field is. Then the join\nof None and Class(k) is Class(k), the fields stay pointers, `is None` is\na null compare, and the method call is direct. What Python requires on\ntop of ZynML is that reading through None raises AttributeError rather\nthan faulting: a null check ahead of each attribute read, write and\nmethod call on a class-typed value (a compare and a branch, against a\nbox and a dispatch today). Everything that consumes a class value has\nto take null: str/repr (prints None), truthiness (None is false), `==`\nand `is`, boxing (null boxes as None), list elements of `Elem::Class`,\nand the operator methods (TypeError).\n\nThe same lattice change keeps a function that returns an instance on\none path and None on another (or falls off its end, c9503d9) at\nClass(k) instead of Object.","files":null}]}